s representative of any issues or delays, but a matter of unprecedented volume,\u0094 the release said, noting that the county\u0092s canvassing board is continuing to examine suspect absentee ballots. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR Overall, President Barack Obama had more than 61 percent of the vote in Miami-Dade, or 521,329 votes, while Mitt Romney had 317,382, or 37.6 percent, according to the election department\u0092s website. The county was plagued Tuesday by embarrassing delays and long lines preventing the state to be called for Obama or Romney. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR Deputy Elections Supervisor Christina White insisted Wednesday morning that an unusually long ballot and high voter turnout, which was 64 percent, was to blame, not a lack of resources. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR \u0093Its not that there were any problems or glitches,\u0094 White told reporters. \u0093It\u0092s about volume and paper left to be processed.\u0094 NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR Miami-Dade elections staffers worked overnight Tuesday and through Wednesday to count some 30,000 absentee ballots that had been dropped off or mailed to the Doral headquarters and county government center in downtown Miami at the last minute. The ballots had to be turned in by 7 p.m. Tuesday to be eligible for counting. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR Teams of staffers counted 10,000 overnight, but still had another 20,000 to review Wednesday afternoon at the Doral headquarters. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR The process is slow and tedious. To start, a dozen elections staffers must electronically compare images of voters\u0092 signatures on absentee ballots with the signatures on the voters\u0092 registration cards. Then the workers must determine whether the ballots have been properly filled out before counting them in scanning machines. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR If staffers reject any ballots, they set them aside for the Miami-Dade canvassing board to make the final call. The board, sitting in an adjacent room, consists of the chair, County Judge Shelley Kravitz, County Judge Andrew Hague, and the county supervisor of elections, Penelope Townsley. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR The elections office must also count all provisional ballots by 5 p.m. Thursday. White said she did not know the total number of provisional ballots. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR Obama won\u0092t lose the lead in Miami-Dade, where his campaign had a massive grass-roots operation. But how the final batch of ballots affects the overall number in Florida remains to be seen \u0097 Obama leads by just over 46,000, according to the state election department\u0092s most recent numbers. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR The race could still be close enough to trigger a recount in Florida, unless it is waived by Romney, who lost the overall election to the president. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR On Wednesday afternoon, the total difference between the candidates hovered just above the .5 percentage point needed to trigger a recount as the final absentee and early voting numbers trickled in across the state. With such a razor-thin margin, no media outlets had called Florida for Obama or Romney. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR Overall in Florida, at least 8,307,749 voters cast ballots for president, just under the number cast in 2008, when Obama won handily. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR In Miami-Dade, so far, it appears the number of voters who cast ballots for president will be just shy of the 864,636 who did so four years ago. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR Lines were so long in some polling places here that the last voter did not leave the West Kendall Regional Library until a few minutes after 1 a.m. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR When polls officially closed at 7 p.m. hundreds of people were still waiting to cast ballots in precincts around South Florida, in an election that was marked by long lines and the occasional problem. Even after the networks called the race for President Obama, people in South Florida remained in line. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHA